<section class="case-lenses"><p class="eyebrow">CONNECTING THE PERSPECTIVES</p><h2>Experience.<br/>Operations.<br/>Strategy.</h2><div><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>01</span>Experience<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Client and prospect interviews explored needs, buying considerations and relationship expectations.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>02</span>Operations<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>The profiling framework gave sales and marketing a shared reference for qualifying prospects.</p></details><details class="lens-detail"><summary><span>03</span>Strategy<b aria-hidden="true">＋</b></summary><p>Customer fit connected the consultancy’s capabilities and positioning to its commercial priorities.</p></details></div></section>
<section class="outcomes" id="outcomes"><p class="eyebrow">OUTPUTS &amp; OUTCOMES</p><h2>What the work made possible.</h2><p>Customer-fit profiles and buyer personas provided a structured basis for prospecting and campaign focus.</p><div class="reflection"><h3>What I take forward</h3><p>Commercial research is more useful when it explains both where the organisation should focus and what makes a customer a good fit.</p></div></section></div><a class="next-case" href={withBase('work/innovation-services/')}><div><p class="eyebrow">NEXT CASE / 01</p><h2>From fragmented programmes to shared ways of working.</h2></div><span aria-hidden="true" class="round-arrow">↗</span></a>
